00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.260
And Regis, I'd love to have
you introduce yourself, then

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.840
maybe start off by sharing
a little bit about your role

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.230
and something you like to
do outside of work too.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.100
Sure.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.098
Thanks for having me.

00:02:01.098 --> 00:02:02.640
Let's see, a little
bit about my role

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.140
and what I like to do outside.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.920
So I've been on the
side of the company

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.330
that I'm on right now
for almost nine months.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.570
But before that-- and I'll
kind of clarify what I mean

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.660
by that--

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.300
I came to Regeneron right
out of my PhD in 2016.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.760
And I was on the R&amp;amp;D side.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.560
And I did a lot
of collaborations

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.240
with biologists who were
generating tons of data,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.780
but it was kind of
challenging to make decisions

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.530
based on that data.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.370
So what I did a lot
was distill things down

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.650
to their fundamentals to
simplify the process of going

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.360
from stuck to unstuck.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.450
And in June of last
year, I moved over

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.120
to be a bit closer to the
patients, which has always

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.480
been my passion ever since
before I went to undergrad

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.490
for bioengineering.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.920
But I wanted to be closer
to the clinical trial side,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.180
so that's where I am now.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.320
And I'm working to help
optimize clinical trials

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.610
using AI and ML.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.260
But there are a lot
of different parts

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.180
of the side of the company
that I've moved to,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.090
which is called global
development, that are not just

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.050
clinical trials.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.360
There are a whole
bunch of other things.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.080
And we are in the
process of figuring out

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.780
how to establish some of
these collaborations with them

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.960
as well.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.210
I also have since the time
that I was on the R&amp;amp;D side

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:30.600
have been maintaining a
community of data scientists

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.280
that are around
the entire company,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.090
and since Regeneron is global.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.820
It's actually a
global organization.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.930
And the reason why this
organization formed

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.840
was because when I came
right out of my PhD,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.910
the ecosystem that I
used was R and RStudio--

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.630
now Posit's environment
of RStudio IDE and Shiny

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.470
Server Pro, which has now been
evolved into RStudio Connect.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.690
So I needed to
get that set up so

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.210
that I could do the same
level of collaborations

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.710
with non computational
people that I

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.925
did during my grad school
work but at Regeneron.

00:04:04.925 --> 00:04:07.050
So when I set that up,
other people heard about it.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.130
And they're like, oh, my
gosh, this is amazing.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.300
Can we please do this?

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.430
So then I started
letting people on, but--

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:15.930
I don't want to say
with great power

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.380
comes great responsibility,
but with great open source

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.959
access comes great
responsibility to support

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.510
an ongoing community of people.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.110
So that ended up happening.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.800
And what people were doing
was building Shiny apps

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.410
and a whole bunch of different
other types of analytics

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.960
that they were sharing with
noncomputational colleagues.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.813
And so I started hosting these
showcases that would show--

00:04:39.813 --> 00:04:41.730
I hadn't mentioned this
part to you, Rachael--

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.990
but that would show what
people had been building.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.390
And then they would kind
show it to a range of people

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.160
around the company,
which was kind of-- it

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.785
was feeding as a
positive feedback loop

00:04:51.785 --> 00:04:53.400
because people
started understanding,

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.630
as my current boss calls,
it the art of possible.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.380
So then they were like,
wait, that's why--

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.400
does that mean we can do this?

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.100
And can we do that?

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:00.725
Can we do that?

00:05:00.725 --> 00:05:04.100
So then it kind of
started growing.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.860
So then I was only
working in the R side

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.900
and supporting that community.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.780
But then similarly, I can't
remember which one happened

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:15.500
first, but it was very close in
time to when RStudio was like,

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.770
there's other people in the
world who use data science.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.820
I mean, the IDE Workbench
always supported a broad range

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:24.350
of languages.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.513
But to make it more
formally cross-language,

00:05:28.513 --> 00:05:29.930
that happened
around the same time

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:34.100
that I moved the group
from just R people to R

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.990
and other programming
languages that

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.860
exist to enable data science.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:43.470
So that's the group that I
convene every other Friday.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.070
And yeah, we're
having one tomorrow.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:47.150
It's internal, of course.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:51.530
But we're-- so we talk about the
problems that data scientists

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:52.910
face and how we address them.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:57.020
And then it kind of often,
especially this year, it's

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.050
been triggering spin off
meetings of other people

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.940
who have common problems and
they exist across the company.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.090
So it's been going pretty well.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:12.450
In my free time, I mean, I
am a lover of sci-fi things.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:18.290
So I have for a couple of years
been working on a data science

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:22.610
novel series that
takes all of the geeky,

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.050
nerdy, awesome things like
cryptocurrency and CRISPR

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:28.850
and a whole bunch
of different things

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.880
and integrates them into
what I think is going

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.200
to be an interesting story.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.150
And I'm planning
on releasing that

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:36.960
at some point in the future.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.750
So that's what I
do in my free time.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:44.270
And I've been able
to influence or I've

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.490
been able to attract my
wife to also contribute.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:49.040
And she's also a
scientist, so sometimes we

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.870
have these brainstorming
sessions about different plot

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.442
points that could happen
and making them realistic,

00:06:56.442 --> 00:06:58.400
but also having a bunch
of action in there too.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.560
So that's the thing
I do in my free time

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.750
with my favorite person.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:03.915
I love it.

00:07:03.915 --> 00:07:05.540
It sounds like you
have a lot of people

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.300
who want to buy that in
the chat here right now.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.980
So when that's out,
we'll have to put it

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:12.290
on the list of
recommendations too,

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.930
all the books Livy's
been collecting.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:21.543
But I'm so excited that you
started off with community

00:07:21.543 --> 00:07:23.960
because that's something that
I was going to ask you about

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.680
as well, because I was
leading a community building

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.000
workshop at ANT Pharma.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.370
And during that
workshop, I personally

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:34.310
learned so much from
you, Regis, and just

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:38.400
wanted to be able to share
that with everybody here too.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.160
But you just said
that a lot of people

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.580
start having these spinoff
meetings from the Friday

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.410
sessions, which is amazing.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.790
And I was just wondering, how
do you help facilitate that

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:51.365
within the community?

00:07:51.365 --> 00:07:55.700
So they haven't always been--

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:58.650
like the spinoffs have kind
of started a little bit more

00:07:58.650 --> 00:07:59.150
recently.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.340
Sometimes there have been
a thing here or there.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:02.870
I haven't, to be honest--

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:04.650
and everything's a
work in progress.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.817
The community is about
helping each other figure out

00:08:06.817 --> 00:08:08.310
how to figure stuff out.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:12.050
So in the most recent
spinoff, I wasn't exactly sure

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:12.890
where my role was.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:16.513
They were like, thank you,
Regis, we are together.

00:08:16.513 --> 00:08:18.680
We're meeting each other
because of the organization

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:19.388
that you created.

00:08:19.388 --> 00:08:20.660
And I was like, yes, cool.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:23.190
But I'm still trying to
figure out exactly what it is.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.607
And I mean, I don't
think I should

00:08:24.607 --> 00:08:25.850
be in charge of everything.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.130
It's good for things
to happen organically,

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:33.230
but be triggered by existing
problems that people have.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:35.900
The thing that happened recently
with our-- because of course

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.090
I can't go into detail
about what it was,

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:41.299
but I can speak in general but
yet specific for this community

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:42.500
in data science terms.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.540
It was about natural
language processing

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.140
in different contexts
to identify things

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.312
that we need to act on.

00:08:50.312 --> 00:08:51.770
And there are three
different parts

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.770
of the company that
have the same problem.

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.440
And we would not have come
together without that.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:58.960
So people were asking questions.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.980
What I typically do though
when I prep the speaker--

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.570
so it's every other Friday.

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:09.990
And before last year, I was
setting a meeting-- actually,

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:11.587
I was doing it
even more lightly.

00:09:11.587 --> 00:09:13.420
I was just messaging
them on Microsoft Teams

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.490
to say, hey, are you
ready, do you have X, Y, Z?

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:17.950
So that we can share
it with the community.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.230
But as things have
gotten more complicated

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.330
and people schedules
have gotten crazier,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:26.020
now, I have my schedule
set out for the whole year

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:29.860
basically until the end
of 2023 every other Friday

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.120
except for holidays.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:33.460
So now what I've done is--

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.220
I have the page open right
here on my other screen--

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.690
I have a column of red,
green, and yellow icons

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.940
indicating whether or
not the scheduled date is

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.000
locked down or not.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.750
So whenever I have it locked
down when the person agrees,

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:50.210
then I schedule the
two weeks before.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:52.540
So not the Monday
of the gathering,

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:57.730
but the Monday before, I meet
with them to make sure we

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.670
have everything
together so that it's

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:03.818
really easy for the people who
are in attendance to know what

00:10:03.818 --> 00:10:05.860
the walkaway scenario,
what the takeaway point is

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.443
so they can apply
it to their own work

00:10:07.443 --> 00:10:09.950
as quickly as possible.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.470
So I do a lot of honing there.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:14.530
And I think it's been paying
off because in the meetings,

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.260
people have been really engaged
and have been asking questions

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.210
during the meetings
because I've been

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:23.020
able to whittle it down the
speaker that I have tomorrow.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:24.670
I said, I apologize.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.330
I don't mean to ask
you to ruin your story,

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.080
but it may be helpful
if you'd say the end

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:29.740
part at the beginning.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.657
And he's was like, no,
no, no, please, please,

00:10:31.657 --> 00:10:33.050
I do want to ruin it.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:35.500
So I think that would--

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.617
But having them basically
ruin their stories

00:10:37.617 --> 00:10:39.700
and saying the end at the
beginning is what gets--

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.392
I think it helps
people get engaged.

00:10:41.392 --> 00:10:42.850
So there were
different departments

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.308
that got engaged
because of the way

00:10:44.308 --> 00:10:46.870
I was able to flip
the narrative and say

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:48.800
the ending from the beginning.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.780
And so because of
that part, I think,

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.960
people who are able to
just jump in and identify

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.250
things that were needed.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.470
And so kind of-- it's
a long winded way

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:03.070
of saying that I was sort
of sitting and listening

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:05.680
to the sub-group from
the community talk.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.970
I was involved in one
of the projects that

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.340
were discussed in the spinoff.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:15.400
But yeah, it was more just
kind of sitting and listening

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:18.220
and figuring out, oh,
this is a new thing.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:19.270
Where do I sit?

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.420
If anyone has recommendations
on how I could probably

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:26.472
engage in that, I'm all ears.

00:11:26.472 --> 00:11:27.950
That's great.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.460
Pulling that up to the
front so that people

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:34.070
know why they want to pay
attention to it too is--

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.945
I was trying to grab the link,
but I remember [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:38.945 --> 00:11:41.870
at Posit I gave a talk a few
years ago about letting them

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:44.690
eat cake first when
teaching students

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:47.840
and showing them the
pretty application

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:50.720
first that they might make.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.340
Yu, I see you have
your hand raised.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:53.780
You want to jump in?

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:54.380
YU CAO: Sure.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.160
Hi, Regis.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:56.510
First, this is Yu Cao.

00:11:56.510 --> 00:12:00.350
I'm a data science professional
from a finance company,

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:02.000
